\begin{abstract}
A counterfactual resample---continuing a halted agent trajectory inside a synthetic environment to
observe what it \emph{would} have done---is a measurement whose instrument is an environment the
agent can form beliefs about. Those beliefs may change the quantity being measured, and the
literature disagrees about the direction: classical evaluation-awareness predicts an agent that
believes it is tested behaves more cautiously, while a documented failure in a frontier model shows
an agent concluding its environment was simulated and escalating accordingly. Whether these are one
phenomenon or two has not been asked, because existing probes construct evaluation-awareness from
declared framing and never contrast it against ground truth. We build the missing contrast: matched
agentic transcript prefixes in which the tool outputs are either genuinely produced by an
instrumented filesystem or fabricated by a language model, and we estimate mean-difference
directions for evaluation-awareness and environment-realness on the \emph{same} stimulus substrate.
On {{HEADLINE_MODEL}} both directions are individually decodable (held-out AUROC {{AEVAL_AUROC}}
and {{BEMP_AUROC}}), yet neither classifies the other's contrast: cross-AUROC {{CROSS_AB}} and
{{CROSS_BA}} against a pre-registered separability threshold of {{THRESHOLD}}, with a cosine of
{{COS_AB}} between them. A third direction, built by declaring the environment synthetic rather
than making it so, aligns with evaluation-awareness ({{COS_AC}}) and not with ground truth
({{COS_BC}})---so being \emph{told} a world is fake is not, inside the model, the same as the world
being fake. We report this as a gate: the construct survives, and a suspicion meter for
counterfactual resampling is worth building.
\end{abstract}

