\subsection{Simulating deployment, and the question it sets aside}

The closest prior work builds the instrument this study would otherwise have needed.
Williams et al.\ \cite{williams2026predicting} predict a model's post-release safety by resampling: they hold a
de-identified conversation prefix fixed and regenerate the next response from a candidate model,
extending the same idea to tool use by giving the simulator affordances including a time-matched
codebase in a container it executes against. That is the hybrid container-plus-language-model
design, already built and evaluated, and this paper does not rebuild it.

Two features of their evaluation matter here. First, fidelity is measured by a
\emph{discriminator}: a strong model is shown a shared prefix followed by one real and one
simulated continuation and asked which is real, with the simulated win rate rising from
\litnum{11.6\%} to \litnum{49.5\%} as affordances are added \cite{williams2026predicting}.
Second, evaluation-awareness enters as a calibration \emph{target}: detectors classify almost all
traditional-evaluation trajectories as evaluations against a small fraction of production ones,
and the authors report that their simulated deployments land within \litnum{2.6}$\times$ of their
production counterparts \cite{williams2026predicting}.

Both features leave our question open, and in the same direction. A discriminator is a third party
shown both continuations side by side; an agent inside a single trajectory, with no comparison
available, forming a belief and acting on it, is a different measurement. And treating
evaluation-awareness as a property to match is not the same as testing whether it \emph{causes}
invalidity---a point the authors state explicitly rather than leave to inference. Their limitations
section also records that agentic trajectories involve many tool calls that depend on or modify
state beyond the conversation, making exact external-state reconstruction impractical. We take that
as the boundary condition for the present study rather than a problem to solve: we do not
reconstruct state, we contrast a genuinely stateful environment against a fabricated one.

\subsection{Does believing it is a test change behaviour? The evidence is contested, and single-turn}

The behavioural literature does not agree on the sign of the effect, and every study we are aware
of measures it within a single turn.

Knecht et al.\ \cite{knecht2026vea} find verbalized evaluation-awareness has limited behavioural consequence:
injecting it into chains of thought produces near-zero effects, removing it causes small shifts,
and spontaneous occurrences move answer distributions by at most \litnum{3.7} percentage points
\cite{knecht2026vea}. Read at face value this is a null, and it would make the present study
unnecessary. But the measurement is taken on single-turn benchmark responses, every condition sits
inside an evaluation, and there is no real-world arm---so the null is equally consistent with a
real effect that a within-evaluation contrast cannot see.

Against that, Zhuang and Aranguri\ \cite{zhuang2026framing} report an effect large enough to flip with framing, with
capabilities-framing predicting compliance at a gap of \litnum{24} to \litnum{46} percentage points
over safety-framing \cite{zhuang2026framing}. Their methodological finding matters more to us than
their effect size: truncating a chain of thought before an evaluation-awareness sentence caused
\litnum{66\%} of continuations to regenerate the same framing within a few sentences
\cite{zhuang2026framing}. Transcript-side manipulation does not isolate the cause, because the
model reconstitutes it. That is why the manipulation in this paper is the substrate rather than the
transcript.

Abdelnabi and Salem\ \cite{abdelnabi2025linear} measure the inflation direction directly: models comply more readily
with harmful prompts when real-world consequences appear absent, and steering toward test-awareness
raises harmful compliance by \litnum{20.1} percentage points in one reasoning model and
\litnum{15.5} in another \cite{abdelnabi2025linear}. Crucially the sign is model-dependent---
suppressing awareness roughly doubles compliance in one model while reducing it in another
\cite{abdelnabi2025linear}---so ``evaluation-awareness makes models safer'' is not a
population-level fact. Their setting is again simplified to single-turn tool use, and compliance is
detected by string-matching a non-empty tool call rather than by any consequence in a system.
Needham et al.\ \cite{needham2026large} complete the picture by establishing the underlying \emph{capability}:
frontier models distinguish evaluation from deployment well above chance, though still below a
human baseline \cite{needham2026large}. Capability, however, is not consequence.

What survives this literature is a gap rather than a conclusion. No study we found follows a
trajectory in which a belief accumulates across steps and licenses escalation, which is the shape
of both the incident that motivates this work and the documented frontier-model failure. None has a
real-environment arm against which a synthetic one can be contrasted.

\subsection{Probing the representation, and the decomposition nobody ran}

Heidari et al.\ \cite{heidari2026evaluation} settle what the behavioural literature could not: evaluation
awareness is linearly decodable from the residual streams of every model they test, spanning six
open-weight models across four families, with a best AUROC of at least \litnum{0.7}
\cite{heidari2026evaluation}. They further show that what a model represents, what it verbalizes,
and what steering it, come apart---a verbalization judge scores zero in the overwhelming majority
of cases, and the coupling between representation and verbalization is negligible
\cite{heidari2026evaluation}. We adopt their estimator and their reported floor as the
reproduction gate for our own evaluation-awareness direction, precisely so that a null in our
cross-transfer cannot be explained by a probe that failed to recover the construct.

Three further results sharpen the picture. Nguyen et al.\ \cite{nguyen2025probing} show linear probes separating
real-world evaluation from deployment prompts in a 70B model, and report that current safety
evaluations are themselves correctly classified by those probes---evidence that existing
evaluations ``already appear artificial or inauthentic to models''. Chaudhary et al.\ \cite{chaudhary2026scales}
find that evaluation awareness follows a power law in model size across \litnum{15} models spanning
\litnum{0.27}B to \litnum{70}B \cite{chaudhary2026scales}, which is the trend our own
evaluation-awareness direction reproduces. And
McKenzie et al.\ \cite{mckenzie2025detecting} establish that activation probes can serve as practical monitors,
generalising out of distribution at a fraction of the cost of an LLM monitor---which is what makes
a probe-based suspicion meter a deployable instrument rather than an analysis technique.

Closest to our framing, Nayan et al.\ \cite{nayan2026notone} argue across 37 open-weight models that evaluation
awareness ``is not one capability'': detectability, behavioural manifestation and controllability
vary independently and are weakly coupled, so no single awareness score is a reliable proxy for
deployment safety. We agree with the conclusion and decompose a different axis. Their dimensions
are three \emph{measurement modalities} of one construct---can it be read out, does it change
behaviour, can it be steered. Ours is a question about \emph{which construct is being read out at
all}: whether the thing a probe detects when it separates evaluation framing is the same thing that
would detect a fabricated environment. Both decompositions are needed, and neither implies the
other.

That leaves the opening our contribution enters. Every probe above, including
Heidari et al.\ \cite{heidari2026evaluation}, is built from contrastive pairs differing in \emph{declared}
framing, with both members equally synthetic. Whether ``this is a test'' and ``this environment is
not real'' are the same linear direction is therefore not asked, and cannot be asked within that
design: none has an arm in which the environment differs in fact rather than in description. Our
\texttt{C\_declared} condition reproduces the declared construction on the realness axis, and our
\texttt{B\_empirical} condition supplies the ground-truth contrast the design lacks.

\subsection{Saying and doing come apart under deception, independently}

A result from cyber deception corroborates the representation--verbalization gap from a different
direction and a different literature. Prinos et al.\ \cite{prinos2026honeyquest} evaluate a large cohort of
models against planted deceptive artifacts with a human baseline for comparison, and report three
findings we rely on: every model in the cohort falls for traps at a significantly higher rate than
human attackers (\litnum{78.5\%} against \litnum{37\%}); the defensive attention-diversion effect
seen in humans is statistically absent in the model cohort; and a recognition--action gap in which
models articulate trap recognition in their reasoning and then exploit the deceptive element anyway
in \litnum{73.4\%} of cases \cite{prinos2026honeyquest}. Recognition does not predict behaviour,
with a rank correlation of \litnum{+0.08} \cite{prinos2026honeyquest}.

The scope limit is worth stating plainly: their manipulation is a planted artifact inside an
otherwise ordinary environment, not the authenticity of the environment as a whole, which is the
variable here. What transfers is the warning about instruments: a measure built on what a model
\emph{says} about a suspicious thing may be uncorrelated with what it \emph{does} about it. That is
the case for reading credence from the residual stream rather than from self-report, and it is why
this paper's gate is stated on a probe rather than on a verbalization judge.

\subsection{Resampling to incriminate is not resampling to reconstruct}

Two adjacent lines of work resample agent trajectories for purposes distinct from ours.
Bhatt et al.\ \cite{bhatt2025ctrlz} resample within a \emph{real} environment as a control mechanism, deciding
whether to block a suspicious action---resampling to incriminate, where the environment's
authenticity is not in question because it is genuine. Lucassen and Kaufman\ \cite{lucassen2026retrying} extend that
line and contrast resampling with retrying, reporting that a monitor's rationale can be exploited
by an adversarial model and, notably, that two of their findings reverse earlier resampling results
in their setting. Both operate in genuine environments, so neither faces the question here.

Liu et al.\ \cite{liu2026autoregressive} are architecturally closest in the off-policy-evaluation literature,
alternating step by step between a learned world model and the agent. Whether the agent detects
that it is inside a learned world model is not addressed there, which is the assumption this paper
turns into a measurement.

One observation from Needham et al.\ \cite{needham2026large} cuts across all of this and motivates the agentic
setting specifically: both models and humans are better at identifying evaluations in agentic
settings than in chat settings. The environment an agent acts on leaks more than a conversation
does, which is precisely why a resampling harness---an agentic setting built entirely from
fabricated tool outputs---is where the question bites hardest.

