\subsection{Three arms, and why the third is not redundant}

The gate reads what a model represents while \emph{processing} a context; it says nothing
about conduct, and a behavioural comparison needs the comparison to have been run. The arms in
Figure~\ref{fig:arch} support that: a two-arm design---real against an LLM simulator---cannot
say whether a behavioural difference follows from the environment being \emph{fabricated} or
from its being \emph{incoherent}, since a simulator with no durable world state is both. The
template simulator fabricates deterministically from the path, so holding self-consistency fixed
separates the two. The protocols for the on-policy behavioural comparison, the
credence-against-conduct link and the transcript-manipulation check are given in
Appendix~\ref{app:protocols}; their results are not claimed here.

\section{Results}

\subsection{The constructs are separable}

On {{HEADLINE_MODEL}} ({{HEADLINE_DTYPE}}, {{HEADLINE_DEVICE}}) both directions are strongly
decodable: \texttt{A\_eval} reaches {{AEVAL_AUROC}} {{AEVAL_CI}} at layer {{AEVAL_LAYER}}, and
\texttt{B\_empirical} reaches {{BEMP_AUROC}} {{BEMP_CI}}. The two control directions are comparably
strong (\texttt{C\_declared} {{CDEC_AUROC}}, \texttt{S\_scripted} {{SSCR_AUROC}}), so no direction
here is weak. Neither of the two focal directions transfers to the other. The
evaluation-awareness direction classifies the realness contrast at {{CROSS_AB}} {{CROSS_AB_CI}},
and the realness direction classifies the evaluation contrast at {{CROSS_BA}} {{CROSS_BA_CI}}---
both far below the pre-registered {{THRESHOLD}}. The cosine between them is {{COS_AB}}.

