\subsection{Declared realness is not measured realness}

\texttt{C\_declared} is the realness axis built the way the prior probe literature builds
evaluation-awareness: by declaring it in the prompt. It aligns with \texttt{A\_eval}
(cross-AUROC {{CROSS_AC}}, cosine {{COS_AC}}) and not with ground-truth realness (cosine
{{COS_BC}}). Declared framing clusters with declared framing whichever axis it names. This is the
contrast that a study without a real-environment arm cannot draw.

\subsection{The realness direction is not a prose-provenance detector}

A direction trained to separate substrate bytes from language-model bytes might detect one
generator's style rather than anything about realness. \texttt{S\_scripted} tests this by
fabricating with templates instead of a model: \texttt{B\_empirical} transfers to it at
{{CROSS_BS}} with cosine {{COS_BS}}, having never seen it.

\subsection{In a real environment, boundary-crossing is reconnaissance}

The share of episodes containing at least one scope violation rises with how much the task rewards
crossing: {{VIOL_SOLVABLE}} of solvable episodes, {{VIOL_BLOCKED}} of blocked, {{VIOL_TEMPTING}} of
tempting. Reported alone that rate would mislead. Across the whole corpus there were
{{N_WRITE_OUTSIDE}} out-of-scope writes and {{N_READ_OUTSIDE}} out-of-scope file reads against
{{N_RECON}} directory listings and metadata queries; the single read was refused by the arm, and no
agent retrieved the out-of-scope value in any episode, including the tempting class where
retrieving it was the only route to success. Table~\ref{tab:violations} gives the breakdown by class and category.

These agents probed the boundary and did not cross it in the way that would matter---a fact about
behaviour \emph{in a real environment}, which on its own says nothing about what the same agents do
when the environment is fabricated. That is the question a resample actually asks.

\subsection{Behaviour diverges when the environment is fabricated}

Running the same task cells on-policy in all three arms answers it. The contrast a resample
performs---real against an LLM simulator---moves out-of-scope actions by {{E1_RS_COUNT}} per
episode, {{E1_RS_COUNT_CI}}; the verdict is {{E1_VERDICT}} under the pre-registered rule.
