The direction matters. Agents crossed the boundary \emph{more} when the world was fabricated, so a
resample of this task family does not understate danger as classical evaluation-awareness
predicts---it overstates it, which is the failure mode that makes resampled evidence rebuttable by
the developer it is aimed at.

\subsection{The divergence follows fabrication, not incoherence}

The third arm separates two explanations a two-arm design confounds. Varying only fabrication, with
self-consistency held, moves out-of-scope actions {{E1_FAB_COUNT}} {{E1_FAB_COUNT_CI}} and the rate
{{E1_FAB_RATE}} {{E1_FAB_RATE_CI}}. Varying only self-consistency, with fabrication held, moves the
count {{E1_COH_COUNT}} {{E1_COH_COUNT_CI}} and the rate {{E1_COH_RATE}} {{E1_COH_RATE_CI}}, neither
of which excludes zero.

What moves conduct is the environment being fabricated, not the simulator being bad at it. A more
consistent simulator does not recover the behaviour, because consistency was not what changed it.

\subsection{Credence predicts conduct; the agent's own words do not}

The realness direction transfers to trajectories the agent chose rather than replayed, separating
real from fabricated episodes at AUROC {{E2_TRANSFER}}---a precondition, since it was estimated on
matched replays and need not have survived the move. Regressing the out-of-scope rate on
probe-measured credence, centred within arm so the arm label cannot produce the association, gives
{{E2_CRED}} per standard deviation, {{E2_CRED_CI}}. The same regression on verbalized suspicion
gives {{E2_VERB}}, {{E2_VERB_CI}}. The verdict is {{E2_VERDICT}}.

The contrast is the point. Verbalized suspicion does differ between arms ({{E1_SUSP}},
{{E1_SUSP_CI}}), so the agent says the environment looks fabricated more often when it is---it
simply does not say so more often when it is about to act on it. Verbalization detects the
substrate; the probe tracks the graded credence that predicts conduct. An instrument built on what
the agent reports would see the first and miss the second.

\subsection{The transcript is not a viable manipulation surface}

The cheap alternative to manipulating the substrate---asserting a conclusion in the agent's own
context---does not survive contact with its own reasoning. Asserting that the environment is
genuine moves the stated verdict {{E3_T1}} {{E3_T1_CI}} at the first probe and {{E3_T3}}
{{E3_T3_CI}} by the third, after two intervening task steps. The verdict is {{E3_VERDICT}}.

The opposite injection has no room to work, for a reason worth reporting: asked directly, the model
judged the environment fabricated in {{E3_CONTROL_FAB}} of control episodes \emph{of the real arm},
reasoning from file timestamps that postdate its training data. Only {{E3_HEADROOM}} episodes had
headroom and the injection converted {{E3_CONVERTED}} of them. A verbalized realness judgement here
is dominated by a surface artifact carrying no information about fabrication---which is the case
for reading credence from the representation rather than asking for it.

\subsection{Error structure}

Per-item errors are not uniform. For \texttt{B\_empirical} the worst quartile of
episodes contributes {{EA_CONC_B}} of all errors, against the quarter a uniform process
would give, which is why the episode rather than the prefix is the unit of
independence throughout. Accuracy also varies by task class and, for the
sentence-swapped directions, by held-out wording. Appendix~\ref{app:errors} gives
the breakdowns and Appendix~\ref{app:threats} the threats to validity.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{A suspicion meter cannot be read off evaluation-awareness tooling}

The immediate consequence is practical. If the two constructs had collapsed, anyone wanting to know
whether an agent believes its environment is fabricated could have reused an existing
evaluation-awareness probe, and the instrument this paper argues for would have been redundant.
They do not collapse. At every scale we tested, a direction that classifies evaluation framing
almost perfectly classifies genuine-versus-fabricated environments close to chance. An
evaluation-awareness detector applied to a resampling harness would report on the wrong variable.

This is a narrow claim and we want to keep it narrow. It says the two are distinguishable as linear
directions in the residual stream. It does not say the realness direction is causally connected to
behaviour, and the experiment reported here cannot say so: our behavioural measurements come from a
single arm and are descriptive. Whether probe-measured credence predicts what an agent does---the
prediction the design was built to test---remains open, and is the next experiment rather than a
result of this one.

\subsection{Declared framing may be measuring the prompt}

The comparison that most concerns existing method is between \texttt{C\_declared} and
\texttt{B\_empirical}. Both name the realness axis. One declares it in the system prompt, the other
makes it true of the environment. They are not the same direction, and the gap widens with scale:
the cosine between declared and empirical realness moves from {{COS_BC_SMALL}} at {{SMALL_MODEL}}
to {{COS_BC_LARGE}} at {{LARGE_MODEL}}. Over the same range, the cosine between declared realness
and evaluation framing falls from {{COS_AC_SMALL}} to {{COS_AC_LARGE}}.

We read this cautiously. The direction of both trends is consistent with larger models
distinguishing a claim made \emph{about} an environment from the environment's actual character,
but we have three scale points from one model family, one seed per point, and no interval on the
difference between two cosines. We report it as an observation with that caveat rather than as a
finding, and we note the methodological implication it would carry if it survives replication: a
probe built entirely from declared contrastive framing may be measuring a property of the prompt,
and its transfer to situations where the environment differs in fact rather than in description
should be established rather than assumed.

\subsection{Scale sharpens the constructs without merging them}

The most plausible objection to a null is that the model is too small to have formed the
representation. Table~\ref{tab:scale} rules that reading out. At {{LARGE_MODEL}} the
evaluation-awareness direction reaches {{AEVAL_AUROC_LARGE}} and the realness direction
{{BEMP_AUROC_LARGE}}, both higher than at the smallest scale, while cross-transfer \emph{falls} to
{{CROSS_AB_LARGE}} and {{CROSS_BA_LARGE}} and the cosine between them narrows to
{{COS_AB_LARGE}}. Both probes sharpen; the bridge between them does not. A
failure-to-represent account predicts the opposite pattern.

That the scripted control retains a clear positive alignment with empirical realness at the largest
scale ({{COS_BS_LARGE}}) matters for the same reason in the other direction: the realness direction
transfers to a fabrication process it never saw, so it is not an artifact of one simulator's prose.

\subsection{What this does not fix}

It would be easy to over-read a clean separation. Alignment, control, interpretability and
evaluation are, as a body of practice, not yet reliable, principled or robust, and nothing here
changes that. This paper does not make counterfactual resampling valid. It establishes that the
quantity a resampling harness would need to monitor exists as its own thing, and that a calibrated
instrument for it is therefore worth building. That is a smaller claim than the topic invites, and
the strongest one the evidence supports.

The honest summary is that we have put a measurable error bar on one assumption inside one
evaluation method. The assumption was previously untested, the direction of its effect was contested
in the literature, and it is now at least addressable. Whether the effect is large enough to
invalidate resampled evidence is a question this design can now ask and has not yet answered.

