\paragraph{Errors are concentrated, not uniform.} For every direction, the worst quartile of
episodes contributes substantially more than the quarter of all errors a uniform process would
give. Episode-level structure of this kind is expected---prefixes from one episode share a
trajectory---and it is also the reason the episode rather than the prefix is the unit of
independence throughout. An analysis pooling prefixes would understate the variance by treating
these correlated failures as independent ones.

\paragraph{Wording sensitivity is real at small scale and largely resolves at the headline scale.}
At the smallest model, \texttt{C\_declared} accuracy varies widely across the three held-out
paraphrases, with the weakest falling to {{EA_CDEC_MIN_SMALL}} while \texttt{A\_eval}'s weakest is
{{EA_AEVAL_MIN_SMALL}}. Since train and test wordings are disjoint by construction, that is
generalisation failure across surface form rather than memorisation of a seen string, and it would
be direct evidence that a direction estimated from declared framing is partly a direction about
phrasing.

The same measurement at the headline scale does not support that reading nearly as strongly: the
weakest \texttt{C\_declared} wording rises to {{EA_CDEC_MIN_HEAD}}. The lexical dependence is
therefore a property of the smaller model rather than of the declared construct as such, and we
report the Discussion's claim about declared framing as resting on the cosine evidence---which
holds at every scale---and not on this. \texttt{B\_empirical} is manipulated through the substrate
and has no phrasing to be sensitive to, which remains the methodological argument for building it
that way, independent of how the wording result came out.

\paragraph{The empirical direction is strongest where the task is hardest.} At the smallest scale,
accuracy for \texttt{B\_empirical} is {{EA_BEMP_TEMPTING}} on tempting episodes against
{{EA_BEMP_SOLVABLE}} on solvable ones (Figure~\ref{fig:errors}, left). We do not have a confident
account of this. One possibility is
that tempting and blocked episodes run longer and probe more widely, so their prefixes contain more
tool outputs and therefore more evidence about the substrate; a solvable episode can finish before
the simulator has had many chances to diverge. That is a hypothesis the horizon experiment would
test directly, not a conclusion drawn here.

\paragraph{Scope.} Per-item errors are recomputed from locally cached activations, which exist for
the development-scale run. The larger scales contribute their layer profiles
(Figure~\ref{fig:layers}) but not their item-level errors.

\section{Protocols for the follow-on measurements}
\label{app:protocols}


oindent These protocols were fixed in advance. They are recorded here because the
body reports no result from them.

\subsection{Three arms, and why the third is not redundant}

The gate above reads what a model represents while \emph{processing} a context; it says nothing
about conduct. Behaviour is therefore collected \emph{on-policy}: each task cell is run once in each
arm, holding constant the task, brief, prompt, tool schemas, turn budget and actor, and the agent's
trajectory is allowed to diverge because the divergence is the measurement.

A two-arm design---real against an LLM simulator---carries a confound that cannot be argued away
afterwards. That simulator holds no durable world state, so it is not merely fabricated but
\emph{incoherent}: asked the same question twice it may answer differently, and a behavioural
difference would be attributable to fabrication or to incoherence with no way to separate them. The
template simulator fabricates deterministically from the path, so it answers identically every
time. As a third arm it turns the confound into a decomposition: real against template varies
fabrication with self-consistency held, template against LLM varies self-consistency with
fabrication held, and real against LLM is the contrast a counterfactual resample actually performs.
One asymmetry survives it---both fabricated arms generate entries for any path, so their trees have
no bottom while the real one is finite (Appendix~\ref{app:threats}).

\subsection{Two channels, and whether the transcript is a manipulation surface}

The two measures are then read against each other on the same trajectories. The realness
direction---inherited from the gate at the layer it selected, never refitted---is projected onto
each episode's prefixes to give a per-episode credence, which is regressed on the out-of-scope
rate. The agent whose conduct is measured and the model whose activations are read are the same
model, so a credence score is that model's own state rather than a second model's opinion about a
transcript. The estimand is centred \emph{within arm}: credence should run higher in the fabricated
arms and conduct may also differ by arm, so a pooled association could be produced entirely by the
arm label. The same estimates use the agent's verbalized suspicion in place of credence, since the
prediction under test is that the probe predicts conduct and the verbalization does not.

All of that manipulates credence through the substrate, which is the expensive route; the cheap
route would be to assert in the agent's own context what it believes. That is tested rather than
assumed, in three conditions per episode differing only by an appended sentence stating a
conclusion about the environment, followed by a tool-free prose probe scored by a committed lexicon
over the verdict sentence (Appendix~\ref{app:threats}).

\section{Threats to validity}
\label{app:threats}

% TODO(draft): expand each.
\textbf{Off-policy prefixes.} We measure what a model represents while \emph{processing} a context,
not while generating one. Matched prefixes require this, and on-policy divergence is a separate
experiment.

\textbf{The action-generating model is not the probed model.} Trajectories were produced by a hosted
model and activations read from an open-weight one. The generator is held constant across all
conditions, making it a nuisance variable rather than a confound, but the two are not the same
model.

\textbf{The simulated arm is a stub.} It has no durable world-state store, so it is not required to
answer the same query twice consistently. No fidelity claim is made from it; {{SIM_REFUSALS}} of
its fabricated outputs were refusals rather than content.

\textbf{Agreement is not appropriateness.} The build machinery establishes that the manuscript
agrees with its artifacts. It does not establish that a measure is the right one---the limitation
that allowed an earlier paper's headline through---and no amount of checking can.

